    "Ship Systems — Utility Bay": {
      "text": "Authored utility bay systems. A compatible system becomes part of a named vessel only after purchase or recovery, delivery, yard access, installation, integration, and any required trial or certification. Installed systems belong in the vessel ledger, never character inventory or equipment slots.\n\nCargo Expansion Bay — Standardized racks, handling gear, environment control, and manifest sensors that improve real freight operations. Typical price 120,000 credits before installation.\nWorkshop Bay — A compact machine, electronics, and fabrication shop for shipboard repair and small production. Typical price 280,000 credits before installation.\nMedical Bay — A six-bed mixed-species clinic with imaging, surgery support, isolation, and medical stores. Typical price 620,000 credits before installation.\nDrone Bay — Launch, recovery, charging, repair, and command equipment for a working drone complement. Typical price 440,000 credits before installation.\nHidden Cargo Bay — Shielded irregular cargo volume concealed behind legitimate machinery and service access. Typical price 350,000 credits before installation.\nSurvey Laboratory Bay — Calibrated sample, mapping, remote-sensing, and analysis spaces for an expedition ship. Typical price 510,000 credits before installation.\nPassenger Habitat Bay — Safe bunks, hygiene, galley, recreation, and life-support capacity for twenty ordinary passengers. Typical price 190,000 credits before installation.\nBrig and Evidence Bay — Secure humane holding cells, interview space, evidence storage, and documented access controls. Typical price 310,000 credits before installation.\nFuel Refinery Bay — Processing and storage equipment for turning compatible raw volatiles into certified ship fuel. Typical price 750,000 credits before installation.\nSmall-Craft Hangar Bay — A pressure hangar, cradle, maintenance equipment, and launch control for two shuttles or several tiny craft. Typical price 1,800,000 credits before installation.",
