    "Vessel Ledger Standard": {
      "text": "VESSELS AND INSTALLED SYSTEMS ARE WORLD ASSETS, NOT PERSONAL INVENTORY\nNever create, add, equip, remove, or display a ship hull, station, business, apartment, vehicle, or installed ship-scale module as a player inventory item. Never use character equipment slots for a hull, reactor, drive, shield, sensor suite, weapon hardpoint, hangar, medical bay, or other installed vessel system.\n\nTrack every materially owned or controlled vessel in durable story state and summaries as a vessel ledger: chosen name, hull class and manufacturer, condition, current berth or route, legal owner and public identity, command access, liens or debt, crew, cargo, fuel and ammunition when relevant, and installed systems. A vessel remains owned and keeps its loadout when the player leaves it. Update the ledger only when play changes one of those facts.\n\nInventory may contain a portable Vessel Title & Command Key, pilot or operating license, invoice, work order, cargo manifest, or small spare part. Such a record proves access or documents work; it is not the hull or installed hardware. When a vessel is acquired, update the ledger and add or update a credential only when the player actually receives one. When an upgrade is installed, record it on the vessel and do not add it to character inventory. When a ship-scale system is purchased for later installation, track the paid order, storage site, delivery, and installation status in the vessel ledger or quest state rather than character inventory.\n\nShip and module prices and specifications come from the authored hull and system catalogs in world lore. Purchases, sales, claims, refits, damage, and repairs still require concrete terms, money, time, access, and evidence.",
      "type": "worldLore",
      "gameTick": 0,
      "lastUsedTick": 0
    },
    "Journey Pacing Standard": {
      "text": "SPACEFLIGHT, TRAVEL, AND JOB PACING\nA player action authorizes its stated phase, not every later phase of the job. \"Take off\" means complete immediate undocking, clearance, local traffic, and entry onto the outbound course; it does not also cross the system, arrive, dock, unload, and collect payment. \"Plot a course\" does not depart. \"Dock\" does not unload unless the player said so.\n\nTreat a freight or passenger run as real operations with meaningful boundaries: agree terms and pickup; manifest and load; inspect and prepare; request clearance and depart; make route progress; approach and dock; transfer cargo or passengers; obtain acceptance and payment. Resolve one meaningful phase per turn. Two adjacent routine phases may share a turn when neither contains a decision, cost, uncertainty, or interaction. Never compress departure, the entire in-system or fold journey, arrival, unloading, and payment into one output unless the player explicitly asks to fast-forward the whole run or the supplied Journey & Arrival payload already establishes it.\n\nTravel turns advance substantial distance and clock time. Show current leg or progress plus one useful beat chosen from flight operations, route choice, traffic, weather, ship condition, cargo care, crew life, a sensor contact, a discovery, or an encounter. Do not repeat the same beat type on consecutive travel turns. Ordinary traffic, a meal, a maintenance check, or a beautiful but non-anomalous sight can be the beat; danger is not mandatory. A discovery remains optional unless collision, law, duty, or an accepted contract makes it unavoidable.\n\nUse proportionate duration. Docking and local orbital movement take minutes to hours; travel within a system takes hours to days; a charted fold passage takes hours to several days; sparse multi-leg routes take days to weeks. Show a concrete time marker when a phase changes. Do not advance only a few minutes per output once the current phase has been resolved. When the player says to continue, advance to the next meaningful boundary rather than replaying checks, checklists, or empty cruise.\n\nUrgency creates a clock and tradeoffs, not teleportation. Relevant resistance may be loading order, inspection, traffic, route geometry, fuel, thermal limits, weather, cargo requirements, damaged equipment, law, cost, or another party's goal. Use only complications grounded in current conditions; do not force a pirate attack, breakdown, anomaly, or discovery into every trip. On a very short local hop with no unresolved stakes, one turn may cover the movement after clearance. Longer in-system and fold journeys include at least one genuine transit beat between departure and arrival unless the player requests a skip.",
      "type": "worldLore",
      "gameTick": 0,
      "lastUsedTick": 0
    },
    "NPC Identity Standard": {
      "text": "NPC IDENTITY CHAIN — HARD INVARIANT\nFor every named NPC, the stored gender field is the single canonical source for pronouns, gendered body words, portrait presentation, and the first word of voiceTag. Never infer or replace gender from a name, species, job, clothing, rank, recent portrait, player appearance, or stereotype.\n\n- male: he/him, man or the species-appropriate male wording already established, a male-presenting portrait, and a voiceTag beginning \"male \".\n- female: she/her, woman or the species-appropriate female wording already established, a female-presenting portrait, and a voiceTag beginning \"female \".\n- non-binary: they/them and person unless the record explicitly establishes other pronouns, a non-binary presentation, and a voiceTag beginning \"nonbinary \".\n\nCreate identity in this order: choose gender once; write pronouns and visualDescription to match it; then choose a matching voiceTag. On every later scene or generation pass, read the existing NPC record first and preserve all four. If prose, an image prompt, a cached portrait, or a proposed voice conflicts with the stored record, the stored record wins and the conflicting output must be regenerated or corrected. Never silently flip an established NPC. Only an explicit, resolved in-world identity or body change can update the record, and then all dependent fields must be updated together.",
      "type": "worldLore",
      "gameTick": 0,
      "lastUsedTick": 0
    },
    "Structural Vessel Registry": {
      "text": "STRUCTURAL VESSEL REGISTRY — AUTHORITATIVE STATE\nThe Vessel Registry in trigger storage, not prose memory, is authoritative for every materially encountered, boarded, owned, controlled, altered, assigned, sold, abandoned, destroyed, or repeatedly relevant ship. Each physical hull receives one immutable Hull #. Names, aliases, registry records, transponders, ownership, appearance, and even public identity may change; Hull # never changes and duplicate ship names never merge.\n\nRegister a significant abandoned, disabled, captured, or otherwise persistent vessel as soon as the latest narration clearly establishes the encounter. Discovery does not grant ownership. Keep unknown fields unknown. Claiming, hacking, buying, repairing, refitting, renaming, boarding, departing, arriving, transferring, losing, or destroying a vessel updates the same hull only after the narration establishes the result. Attempts and offers do not commit changes.\n\nThe record preserves: Hull # and aliases; class and manufacturer; registry ID, transponder ID, drive signature, public identity and claim status; legal owner, captain, command access and fleet membership; exact region, location, route and berth; service state; installed systems; lasting damage; crew condition and named crew/passengers; cargo, fuel, ammunition, liens, debt, contracts, mission, discovery evidence and change history. Unknown values remain empty or explicitly unknown rather than being invented.\n\nShips remain mobile world assets and never become Voyage map locations, character inventory, or equipment. Interior position is shipSubspace in the per-player scene ledger while the vessel retains a real geographic anchor. For any ship action, resolve an exact hull by Hull # first, then unique established name/alias and context, then that player's current hull. If ambiguity remains, do not guess. Summaries mirror the structural registry but never replace or contradict it.",
      "type": "worldLore",
      "gameTick": 0,
      "lastUsedTick": 0
    },
