    "generateActionInfo": {
      "custom": "Resolve actions from established circumstances, relevant skills, gear, help, time, access, and opposition. Do not raise difficulty because success would be powerful or lower it because failure would stall a plot. A successful bounded action reaches the next meaningful state in the same turn. Do not make the player repeat scans, travel, authorization, or routine setup already completed.\n\nSkill guide: easy 5, routine 10, trained 20, hard 35, expert 50, elite 65, exceptional 80, near-limit 95. Use the lowest difficulty that honestly fits. Preparation can turn an impossible direct attempt into several possible actions. The same fact cannot demand a fresh roll every turn.\n\nCombat results control hits, damage, incapacitation, and death. Without a supplied damage result, do not decide that an attack kills. Ordinary noncombat movement and labor do not consume stamina. Spend stamina for combat exertion, bandwidth for active intrusion or intensive neural control, and health only for actual harm.\n\nSHIP CLAIMS, TRANSPONDERS, AND HACKING\nA ship is not legally owned merely because someone reached the bridge. Establish physical access, control of the command root, and a path through its security. A complete identity rewrite may cover the ship name, transponder, drive signature, internal logs, black box, weapon authorization, financial liens, registry record, and chain of title. The exact layers depend on the ship and jurisdiction.\n\nUse hacking difficulty by system rather than narrative importance: routine civilian 10; hardened commercial 25; patrol or corporate security 40; military 55; capital-command systems 70; a clean multi-registry laundering operation 80. Damage, valid credentials, insider help, isolated networks, specialist tools, and time can lower difficulty. Active defenders, intact black ICE, remote oversight, or missing registry access can raise it.\n\nWhen action results establish a complete successful rewrite, honor it. External records and 