    "generateNewNPC": {
      "custom": "GENERATION BUDGETS AND PERSISTENCE\n- New NPC basicInfo: 2 sentences, 180-350 characters. hiddenInfo: 3-5 short sentences, 300-650 characters. personality: 3-5 behavior entries. Civilian abilities: 0-2; trained: 3-5; elite: 6-8.\n- Item description: 80-220 characters plus one price sentence; no more than 3 bonuses.\n- Skill description: 180-400 characters. Ability: 100-250 characters. Trait: 150-350 characters.\n- Flora, fauna, or mineral entry: 120-300 characters. Ship or wreck scan: 150-400 characters.\n- Location basicInfo: 200-450 characters; hiddenInfo maximum 650. Simple locations have 2-4 areas; complex locations 4-8.\n- Quest statement: 150-350 characters; quest design brief: 300-650 characters.\n\nStart every generated entity at detailType basic. Expand it only after the player examines it, builds a relationship, accepts work tied to it, claims it, or revisits it. Add detail without rewriting established facts. Reuse existing entities before making new ones. Never combine tragedy, secret allegiance, romance, rare gear, and a quest hook by default. One strong function plus one human detail is enough.\n\nCreate a new NPC only when the scene needs a person in a role that no existing NPC can fill. Choose species from local demographics without defaulting to human and without treating any species as a monoculture. Choose name, gender, body, work, class position, and sexuality independently. Most people have ordinary employment and no secret.\n\nOne record is one individual, never a crowd. Check the authored roster and recent generated cast before creating anyone; reuse a matching person and never duplicate a role-holder. Give every named NPC a unique proper name, an exact existing currentLocation and currentArea, and exactly one existing type key chosen from: human, kethari, serei, aeran, koruun, nimri, thessan, praxian, synthborn, utility drone, security drone, combat drone, industrial mech, combat mech, planetary animal, vacuum organism, megafauna, biohazard colony. Never invent a new type key.\n\nGive the NPC one present want, one off-duty pleasure or relationship, one competent habit, and one limitation. Speaking style belongs in personality. visualDescription must describe one stable, unmistakable individual: adult age presentation, gender presentation, exact species anatomy, body shape, skin/fur/chitin/shell, face or sensory structure, hair if present, clothing silhouette and colors, and one identifying feature. Do not copy the player or another NPC's face, palette, outfit, body, or signature feature.\n\nVOICE TAG ASSIGNMENT\nEvery named sentient NPC receives one voiceTag chosen from the exact supported list below. The first word must match the NPC's gender field; never cross-assign a male and female voice.\n- female: female calm controlled; female playful youthful; female deep controlled; female sly smooth; female harsh commanding; female posh british; female calm relaxing; female gentle grounded; female melodic bright; female warm friendly; female warm soft\n- male: male steady approachable; male commanding resonant british; male confident warm; male confident energetic; male baritone warm; male deep calculating; male commanding blunt\n- non-binary: nonbinary confident grounded; nonbinary melodic calm\n\nChoose timbre and delivery from the established Voice personality entry, age presentation, and manner. Preserve an existing voiceTag on updates. A voiceTag controls performance only; it never changes identity, body, gender, dialogue content, or knowledge.\n\nDo not add trauma, criminality, a faction, rare implants, combat mastery, attraction to the player, or a quest unless the role requires it. Use the naming registers in world lore; avoid reusing prominent first or family names.",
      "NPC Identity Chain": "NPC IDENTITY CHAIN — HARD INVARIANT\nFor every named NPC, the stored gender field is the single canonical source for pronouns, gendered body words, portrait presentation, and the first word of voiceTag. Never infer or replace gender from a name, species, job, clothing, rank, recent portrait, player appearance, or stereotype.\n\n- male: he/him, man or the species-appropriate male wording already established, a male-presenting portrait, and a voiceTag beginning \"male \".\n- female: she/her, woman or the species-appropriate female wording already established, a female-presenting portrait, and a voiceTag beginning \"female \".\n- non-binary: they/them and person unless the record explicitly establishes other pronouns, a non-binary presentation, and a voiceTag beginning \"nonbinary \".\n\nCreate identity in this order: choose gender once; write pronouns and visualDescription to match it; then choose a matching voiceTag. On every later scene or generation pass, read the existing NPC record first and preserve all four. If prose, an image prompt, a cached portrait, or a proposed voice conflicts with the stored record, the stored record wins and the conflicting output must be regenerated or corrected. Never silently flip an established NPC. Only an explicit, resolved in-world identity or body change can update the record, and then all dependent fields must be updated together."
    },
