      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Ghost> Ghost finishes his half of the ration brick and the warm grain pouch, stows the packaging in the bin, and runs a pre-contact check — sensors, shield generator, airlock cycle — while the autopilot ticks down the leg.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Pike> Pike straps loosely into the jump seat and pulls a handheld ultrasonic scanner from his kit, thumbing it through a battery check before resting it on his knee.\n\nPike: [gravelly] Scanner's charged, spare cell's good. She'll read through a hull skin to about two meters — enough to tell a sealed crate from an empty rack before I cut anything.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Ghost> Two hours slide past in silence. The starfield thickens ahead, and at the three-hour mark the passive array picks up the first hard returns — tumbling metal, a scatter of cold cargo pods, and one large mass dead ahead holding a slow rotation.\n\nPike: [gravelly] There's our girl. That big return's got to be the Carrow. Broke spine means she'll be in two or three pieces, so watch which piece you anchor to.\n\nNarrator: <arrival> The Vesper Nine crosses the debris perimeter and the field opens around them — fractured plating, venting gas plumes, and the sheared fore and aft sections of the Orison freighter drifting in a slow, tangled orbit.\n\nGhost: [flatly] Got her. Aft section's the one with the cargo bay — sealed and intact from what passive shows. I'll set us an anchor point off her flank and you can suit up while I hold position.\n\nPike: [gravelly] Aft section it is. Give me ten minutes on the suit and I'll be ready to step out the moment you're parked.",
