      },
      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Pike> Pike sets the ultrasonic scanner on the seat's tray, unstraps, and pulls his hardsuit from the airlock locker. He works through it methodically — legs first, torso seal, ring locks checked one by one.\n\nPike: [gravelly] Which flank are you anchoring to, pilot? I need to know so I can plan my first push to the aft bay.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Ghost> Ghost eases the Vesper Nine alongside the aft section's dark flank, nudges the thrusters until the hull fills the viewport, and lets the scan sweep run its contracted course while he studies the wreckage through the glass and the sensor returns.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Ghost> The job scan builds its sweep grid cleanly — positions, drift vectors, debris density, all logged for the Guild file. But the visual reads are a mixed bag. The aft section's spine is sheared clean, and the cargo bay doors are sealed, all right — but a hairline fracture runs along the bay's upper seam, and a faint frost bloom on the plating suggests atmosphere bled out slowly rather than all at once. Whatever killed the section, it wasn't a clean break.\n\nPike: [gravelly] That frost line's worth noting. Slow vent means the interior might still hold pressure in pockets — or might not. I'll trust the torch and my suit before I trust any door.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Ghost> Ghost anchors the Vesper Nine to the aft flank with a magnetic grapple, holding station while the sweep completes. The fracture, the sealed doors, and a cold transponder dead since the break all go into his notes — enough for the Guild scan, not yet enough to say what happened aboard.",
